    "text": "### Incident Diagnosis: T13\n\n**evidenceStatus**: insufficient_evidence\n\n**Root Cause Analysis**:\nThe current evidence for the payments service checkout timeout consists solely of the symptom \"request exceeded timeout\" and a general observation that payment requests are slower. We lack pool metrics, provider status codes, and information regarding recent deployments. Consequently, it is impossible to distinguish between several known historical failure modes that present with similar latency or timeout characteristics in the payments service.\n\n**Observed**:\n- Service: payments, environment: production.\n- Symptom: Increased request latency leading to \"request exceeded timeout\" logs.\n- Status: No telemetry available for database connection pools, provider responses, or deployment history.\n\n**Historical Matches**:\n- **Connection Leak (HIST-PAY-POOL, 2026-10-09)**: Historically, unreleased database connections in payment handlers led to 100% pool occupancy and timeouts. This was resolved by deploying a cleanup patch after manual restarts provided only temporary relief.\n- **Upstream Quota Limit (HIST-PAY-QUOTA, 2026-10-18)**: Historically, payment providers returned HTTP 429 and Retry-After headers, causing timeouts. This was addressed by implementing bounded retries with jitter and respecting rate-limiting headers.\n\n**Unverified Details**:\n- It is unknown if the current timeouts are caused by internal resource exhaustion (connection leaks) or external dependency throttling (upstream quotas). We cannot confirm or rule out either hypothesis without metrics.\n\n**Hypotheses**:\n- []\n\n**Recommended Non-Destructive Checks**:\n1. **Check Pool Telemetry**: Inspect current database connection pool occupancy metrics for saturation.\n2. **Check Provider Logs**: Query outgoing request logs to identify HTTP status codes returned by the external payment provider (specifically looking for 429 or 5xx codes).\n3. **Check Deployment History**: Verify if any recent code changes or configuration updates were deployed to the payments service immediately preceding the onset of timeouts.\n4. **Check Thread/Resource State**: Examine service thread dump or resource utilization metrics to distinguish between blocked processing threads and queued requests.",
